      "math_academy_id": "q-110536",
      "knowledge_point_id": "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required",
      "problem": "Given that ${e}^{y+1}-1=3,$ then $y=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y+1}-1 & =3 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y+1} & =4 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =ln⁡(4) \\\\ y+1 & =2ln⁡(2) \\\\ y & =2ln⁡(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=2ln⁡(2)-1.$",
